#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124241 :question/id #uuid "225b0bca-3520-58ab-bd36-0e3de337b604" :question/math-academy-id "e-3068" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124242 :question/id #uuid "70bcf628-cf1f-5da0-85b7-19d0f514ccd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3069" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124243 :question/id #uuid "75c0deaf-38a7-5a81-afac-cae2978d52b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3070" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $$\n\\displaystyle f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},\n$$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)=4\\,i+\\pi \\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bf0df7e-022b-5418-9924-365a7162eff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321611} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa3c59a-6624-5598-a1a5-f291f19779f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321648} {:answer/id #uuid "44d2ebf6-d34d-5c73-86a9-e87ea0a289de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321692} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6fc4ed-289d-56da-b773-3e27dbdc2b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322372} {:answer/id #uuid "bd68ef18-667b-55d2-a107-cbd29381e44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d2ebf6-d34d-5c73-86a9-e87ea0a289de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321692} :answer-field/id #uuid "090efca4-d7f7-51f0-927e-9f82cbd619ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "63eca657-711b-57df-bd85-bce2f11b802d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36216" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=3.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6x-y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,3) & =6(2)-(3)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0315c355-94e8-5b85-bc0d-c26e707e74cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{4}{5}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321176} {:answer/id #uuid "9db2c19a-f9cc-5cd6-8a22-46dc603af896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322389} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f0e-14b9-56f8-a702-fc4364be3a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322605} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c279f3-89f6-5f4b-b92c-736849d41a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322911} {:answer/id #uuid "f880ea2c-47c4-55a4-afa7-5ab8c96b72f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{2}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9db2c19a-f9cc-5cd6-8a22-46dc603af896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322389} :answer-field/id #uuid "36f4a259-af06-543c-939e-9833986d33ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "491a90b9-f33c-5937-8491-8e8447d7cf82" :question/math-academy-id "q-36219" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{6xy+{y}^{2}}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =\\frac{3(1)+(2)}{\\sqrt{6(1)(2)+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{5}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd26c8b-fb1c-5766-9fbe-ab3b0f2403f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=2-2x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514935} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbc826e-b561-565f-a693-c12cb77c5362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515003} {:answer/id #uuid "940c73e1-fac8-5d96-a4d7-4f31a5f433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515186} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab403d6-b2c8-5db7-ac14-47e28ec612f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=2x-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515199} {:answer/id #uuid "d540c582-ba40-5073-81c9-81443650b392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1, \\\\ z &=4x-4\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "940c73e1-fac8-5d96-a4d7-4f31a5f433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515186} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec17033d-7e32-53b3-b00f-f4c5e370a468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "faf15e19-a012-50bb-842f-791131393cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36369" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3x+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)} & =3(1)+6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =3(x-1) \\\\ z & =3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ec35ef-bc6e-5ae3-be18-9b9ce4679dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321669} {:answer/id #uuid "8bdb8a5e-3b0d-5134-98cf-e35ce5f13f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322265} {:answer/id #uuid "a0be3297-c3f7-5b45-9e19-ac84539d01ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322415} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dab9bc-d4e9-5357-b42b-704f2f3b619c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322559} {:answer/id #uuid "feac73a2-6cf2-5fa6-aa53-e2dafe29b2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feac73a2-6cf2-5fa6-aa53-e2dafe29b2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323157} :answer-field/id #uuid "307ef176-8c07-5616-a330-828891c316a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "38a2ac51-d5be-54ed-b565-78bd1a3a67e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-36371" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-3 \\\\ z &=6x-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb9cb29-68e3-5973-a4a4-8242c4c3bfdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122523} {:answer/id #uuid "2d162824-da2b-546d-9abb-654bfdacfdb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122524} {:answer/id #uuid "520b12a4-9c6f-59fa-92da-2250edd1ff5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122525} {:answer/id #uuid "59570931-e9e1-5343-a190-74e25d21e6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122526} {:answer/id #uuid "76e3e537-863d-50f7-922d-42e905ecbbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122527}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bf52da3a-42bd-52d4-baf6-4f9b1486ac5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122522}] :question/id #uuid "76fa0a1f-c88e-5c19-9675-405dad1d4057" :question/math-academy-id "q-36463" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z = f(x, y)$. The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y = 0$. Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\frac{π}{2}$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{x} = y\\cos x + \\cos y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25cf98fa-a5b3-5e6b-b710-84fa1db64ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407304} {:answer/id #uuid "5de2add0-6423-5754-a07f-96730ef51cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407307} {:answer/id #uuid "bccf173f-8570-55d3-b189-673a90a1c377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407310} {:answer/id #uuid "db135884-f54c-58f1-b6d6-52b5e9137f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407312} {:answer/id #uuid "fd44ef3e-d356-53fe-b14e-c4931ae0f4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25cf98fa-a5b3-5e6b-b710-84fa1db64ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407304} :answer-field/id #uuid "a315569d-b4ce-597c-a11e-e51ae4013a54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecca0679-247f-506b-95a3-b2be27b80569" :question/math-academy-id "q-64972" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,-1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}(2xy-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,-1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1) & =(1)^{2}(2(1)(-1)-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,-1) & ={f}_{y}(1,-1)(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3y-3 \\\\ z & =-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,-1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-3y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-3y-1$ (or $-3y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-3,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j-3\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45a98813-2da9-54b1-be36-e284f308a481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266767} {:answer/id #uuid "51b0aabb-8305-5c29-9b5f-9de63ccafeb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266874} {:answer/id #uuid "727b509d-e810-5caf-ac73-b9babe4fc8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267148} {:answer/id #uuid "9da497a9-5263-520b-9a2e-92a6d26e637b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267563} {:answer/id #uuid "a70b4400-cd37-5893-85fa-f7497e058575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45a98813-2da9-54b1-be36-e284f308a481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266767} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf81380-c294-5e58-9e12-11b8701e5c19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea3a7950-5517-53eb-8054-3498346f46a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132579" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+xy$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226ad3f6-7e7b-5847-9314-07159469d3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266452} {:answer/id #uuid "34c500a5-2bfc-5d7d-af3c-b5668516b746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266606} {:answer/id #uuid "97cf4b27-8cb2-5296-96fe-683228875813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267503} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bb424a-1a21-5451-bf5c-bf751e9a4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267641} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ddca65-ba60-5a83-84d5-38e7eeab917a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "226ad3f6-7e7b-5847-9314-07159469d3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266452} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9bb7d99-021d-5d65-952d-0bbc4117f169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "09f5edfe-2ef7-5aa3-b9a3-3d13ae59dd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-132584" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(1,-3,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-3,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-3) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(-3)^{2}}+(-3)\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-3(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106c62f5-6a15-5356-9819-bac29ddd8e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4x+9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266282} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdcc8ed-799e-5868-8d9c-e5cddb211a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4x+21\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266824} {:answer/id #uuid "502e00e2-2746-5fc9-8240-98511ddcf672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=2x+9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266856} {:answer/id #uuid "50dcaed5-8a14-56a6-9c8d-e4ff85b79e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=-2x+15\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266866} {:answer/id #uuid "9b80ced7-aa68-5bf4-8dc4-6402f10538d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=2x+21\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cdcc8ed-799e-5868-8d9c-e5cddb211a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4x+21\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266824} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d228b2-8aea-57a5-b2ca-483e7c7b3e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "484f8b36-97b4-5e4f-a875-d05f61c48bf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132585" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Given that the point $P(4,-2,5)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y\\sqrt{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(4,-2,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(4,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)} & =(-2)\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(4,-2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}(x-4) \\\\ z-5 & =-4(x-4) \\\\ z & =-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,-2,5)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4x+21\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0839792e-52f8-55c0-96a0-793207bc2a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=6x-5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266218} {:answer/id #uuid "1f23aed8-c0d3-5c83-bb76-cf7ec415c01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=3x-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266413} {:answer/id #uuid "af853526-8839-56ef-bbab-ba5bdb85abf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=6x+3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267724} {:answer/id #uuid "d3488bb1-cb08-55c3-baf7-71b2ba5e7513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=3x-5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268028} {:answer/id #uuid "e94121d6-a767-5df0-af24-04d3cf8908a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=4x+3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0839792e-52f8-55c0-96a0-793207bc2a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=6x-5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266218} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc73d7a-8739-5705-bbef-4b94db05673c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c434702-618c-5a71-8a4a-aadc6bd7fee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132587" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2xy+2$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)(2)+2 \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =6(x-1) \\\\ z & =6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=6x-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3535f187-54bf-56fb-8443-7dab32a9c79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=2y-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425099} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c97aa-3243-5467-ac12-075c53d9654e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=y+3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425100} {:answer/id #uuid "43046f4c-8850-5dea-9f71-c5e3ebec2500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=2y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425101} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8e160b-491c-5c8e-996d-6c8ececaab38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=y-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425102} {:answer/id #uuid "77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=y+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=y+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425106} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab8e018-d349-5c74-b2e8-1930a7b5eded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c5dd738-1c94-548e-aa53-3b5d32dc4abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132589" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=y+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b49c0a3-842a-5e4f-8c9f-bcdacd6482db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=x+2\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266525} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa233f4-64d9-5e7c-9bf4-5d6de3431a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=2x-2\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266718} {:answer/id #uuid "5722d450-4de6-5584-a7a4-4c5a28d785ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=-2x+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266918} {:answer/id #uuid "8af7c44c-aec2-5a26-b0b4-846d06ad93e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=x-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267389} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f58a5e-b636-567f-a4e0-4e5efca66f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=-2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5722d450-4de6-5584-a7a4-4c5a28d785ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=-2x+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266918} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a7709cb-f264-5087-8141-9df67bddfa9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90718d28-70a4-5587-a36e-fa57ec8d26d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132590" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{xy}{\\pi }$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(2)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}(x-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\ z-2\\pi  & =-2(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ z & =-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=-2x+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499732} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8f64a9-ff65-58b8-9065-4abeb8498b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=2y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499859} {:answer/id #uuid "8405b123-1df2-58c7-a42b-133a4390e156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=2y+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499905} {:answer/id #uuid "89e9a579-0e60-5841-a0a0-a7fc520c336b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499923} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5f83b9-acd0-55f1-b0be-802d7971c9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499732} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e7d076-c660-5037-a4ff-af8a315c239c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22a41e65-3d40-52de-a0eb-a4c7353c0c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-132593" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d6e4604-362d-57a9-a29b-e6f955c20002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=\\frac{3}{2}y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499720} {:answer/id #uuid "7344417b-833c-54ed-9d9c-2ee1f6469356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499866} {:answer/id #uuid "821cfb95-3293-5dd2-ae19-a95141c69336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{1}{4}y-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499901} {:answer/id #uuid "a05b6b84-89fc-51b8-b22e-5a5a84b42efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499964} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fb22f8-31af-560a-a251-6124b9e4d0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{2}y+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7344417b-833c-54ed-9d9c-2ee1f6469356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499866} :answer-field/id #uuid "92a71cca-98d2-5245-a4d1-fd31406052e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac300975-45c9-5a2c-a8b5-5d2addf48a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-132595" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1843e8dd-af0c-51f7-a63f-d47f4be0aaaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266343} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae2b17b-4d19-5b24-bc0c-59e77736ad05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267387} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b280b-d5f7-568a-8d81-2dd2a22c34a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267583} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0faa48-7712-5417-80bf-a7c921e7985b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268366} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc676ee-4730-534b-a0c7-fe2034b61a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1843e8dd-af0c-51f7-a63f-d47f4be0aaaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266343} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba3e928-a7c0-5873-a82b-0e92f1edc942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7da12630-1fa6-595d-a465-5095a1603e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132599" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y-1}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=5x-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd176d1-71e6-5d2d-a259-a3fcc0af991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266962} {:answer/id #uuid "62df8b67-d01e-577d-9807-c0b20a784a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267013} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8b3ad7-9956-5469-b111-e37bf7bc1bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267549} {:answer/id #uuid "b0142210-122c-5cb4-9c32-769b018c4a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267729} {:answer/id #uuid "dcebd8e5-4a98-5a88-b29f-0e1f28a253c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0142210-122c-5cb4-9c32-769b018c4a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267729} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9c4369a-8e31-5a0a-8b79-02cda6e1bfbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "86de2721-fbfb-53fc-8404-fca3429d9ecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132601" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=2y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j+2\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72b4af52-0cb5-5170-b305-ddbac150686d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267149} {:answer/id #uuid "a7393b59-39d3-5aad-8cbc-5f6ab101035b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267660} {:answer/id #uuid "bc72c95f-ce36-52f6-88e0-ea6a281e11ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267831} {:answer/id #uuid "c452d620-354c-5b6f-b5ca-b7e2e4c22b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267897} {:answer/id #uuid "dfecef19-f61e-5834-b586-b5f0a09dbb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfecef19-f61e-5834-b586-b5f0a09dbb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268121} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75921e8-74b4-5a5e-9fcb-280d1ebead77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecd11e65-1ebe-5f6c-b826-3c8a8ba99741" :question/math-academy-id "q-132602" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,7)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a781070-386b-5885-ae90-e1131fceb047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266514} {:answer/id #uuid "36c5d600-802b-585f-b814-467c86175273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266615} {:answer/id #uuid "897a6535-e50c-5904-94d0-9f0a5507508b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267366} {:answer/id #uuid "e1546c65-4c85-51ab-b887-32752413d5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268132} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb2a519-673a-5b66-a103-b6c0fb7b7e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2j-3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a781070-386b-5885-ae90-e1131fceb047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266514} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf130c40-9ba8-59b2-9288-24b063913d80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e644a49d-2ffc-52d7-91ba-f94a7b4d9023" :question/math-academy-id "q-132604" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "204035e4-46d4-534d-ba05-15ce0e052f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=4y+8\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321387} {:answer/id #uuid "5b70aad1-ccca-5779-affa-e5700fd0cc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321868} {:answer/id #uuid "7510c1fa-11fc-530d-ac02-4cbbe0845097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4y+8\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322059} {:answer/id #uuid "85c6028a-d0f8-58ef-9cb9-0c6994f1659c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=4x+8\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322206} {:answer/id #uuid "9cadbd18-60fa-56f2-aef5-823c9dd90a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=4y-8\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7510c1fa-11fc-530d-ac02-4cbbe0845097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4y+8\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322059} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b4da25-e31a-52b9-9b49-54d616020d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "daecb367-4754-591f-93db-eaa083b20ffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-36301" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,0,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+4y+x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,0,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)} & =(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-8 & =-4(y-0) \\\\ z & =-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,0,8)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4y+8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a88646-da7b-5cc5-a6ca-e3694eb78578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321182} {:answer/id #uuid "31748a7a-7455-5dc9-aebc-92650c97c472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=4x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321526} {:answer/id #uuid "a14281d6-156f-5f48-beed-65acb59f3aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4y-12\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322421} {:answer/id #uuid "af9c34b4-7ca6-552a-a577-8a9187d131ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=-4y+12\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322525} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f4b2b7-738a-52b8-9ea5-229baa45ac6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4y-4\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0f4b2b7-738a-52b8-9ea5-229baa45ac6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4y-4\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b9822b-8388-5077-b4c1-9ebc7d355fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12d0b28e-9c11-5054-bcb2-feb86bd6fc4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36441" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Given that the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(0.5,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡((0.5)(2))+\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =4(y-2) \\\\ z & =4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0.5,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4y-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9a56e0-5972-5209-9d5c-ea16e82e995f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333100} {:answer/id #uuid "66686a01-21cc-56d7-951a-bacb93258c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333921} {:answer/id #uuid "79013707-fe29-522d-ac8d-ac18b46d1880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334075} {:answer/id #uuid "989e0c9d-3b24-5671-9e42-b86c26764caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334342} {:answer/id #uuid "b3886518-c45d-5987-8dfe-39a52ed04936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66686a01-21cc-56d7-951a-bacb93258c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333921} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a4e3654-a019-5e30-92b9-ed3bae770d8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1f6c71da-f5b0-5c03-871d-e07fb324730c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64968" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d882919-8d13-50f7-840d-8a1f45fcd980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333253} {:answer/id #uuid "543ab52d-1623-54f6-b166-c0e43d208801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333756} {:answer/id #uuid "a803a6f4-b31b-5b47-92f1-472e09aa720a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334459} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b4881-8db8-5a4e-a73c-944bf83e8664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335177} {:answer/id #uuid "fa97ca9a-10f2-55a2-b950-5d1537242daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b4881-8db8-5a4e-a73c-944bf83e8664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335177} :answer-field/id #uuid "234510dd-0314-5bd3-949a-5dd7a7a7bea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "725a1fb6-c62e-5bb7-9fe8-49c03c5250ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-64971" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2(x+y)-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=2x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-2\\,k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-2\\,k)=i+2\\,k$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d5f7cf-613e-525d-93c2-0c7080720d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336530} {:answer/id #uuid "6b07d985-3564-5d0a-b070-9e5e048515e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336980} {:answer/id #uuid "c6298338-e8f0-5052-8bae-ef3ddf73ce7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337789} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ad9eb0-f197-5640-819b-488c340e4e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338046} {:answer/id #uuid "f52f84db-f262-57d1-9c5e-5a630266362b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6ad9eb0-f197-5640-819b-488c340e4e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338046} :answer-field/id #uuid "7598b8df-f496-507a-9973-2189f2324d0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6efe9457-81ed-5961-99c6-f2de702a7e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-71198" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =3(2)^{2}(1)-6(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =12-12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c5ba22e-dbcf-5fa2-95ae-a8ddaa66f720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=6y-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425107} {:answer/id #uuid "8020f82d-21fe-5f0d-b435-1b1601809556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=4y-5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425108} {:answer/id #uuid "9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=2y-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425110} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d9df8e-4012-5e27-bf02-9250e930e1d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=4y-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425111} {:answer/id #uuid "f37ea42d-7c61-5d99-8c24-718ac347ab47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=2y-5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=2y-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425110} :answer-field/id #uuid "335b522d-b8a5-5ac7-8b9d-2985076f5c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3868b943-72a6-5a73-9952-d94521bce0ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-71209" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-1 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=2y-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156f0e60-a655-5cee-ace2-00f94feeb9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336234} {:answer/id #uuid "3998953b-84b0-5889-81f7-4dc1224db93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336573} {:answer/id #uuid "4603346d-02b6-5575-9939-64549e4f33fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336681} {:answer/id #uuid "6b358259-0538-5cb3-840e-3b6005519fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=-x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336981} {:answer/id #uuid "ef59035a-1a91-53fe-8a72-a5d6bc5d3dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3998953b-84b0-5889-81f7-4dc1224db93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336573} :answer-field/id #uuid "67c2ebb0-7f04-5390-9b3f-6c5b9ac02ee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceda561f-8269-5197-b05a-efc41af9a9a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71218" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0284616a-7c89-5ca0-af71-91a32977c6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336053} {:answer/id #uuid "208d69f2-61c9-5cef-ae67-df4b82077458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336339} {:answer/id #uuid "4733129a-0c72-56e1-9873-d6951efbe108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336692} {:answer/id #uuid "54939340-4ca9-5a82-b24c-2542ed64af8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336814} {:answer/id #uuid "fb04c736-1e93-5392-937a-e6b52b89f395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0284616a-7c89-5ca0-af71-91a32977c6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336053} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee8ed97-cdf9-56b8-9f86-2a9258c8b74b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2d6bbe53-bb73-5d24-b77f-7c5fef3fe5bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71220" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-6,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-y-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a4a9dc-c717-5035-ba3e-987961f9ee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336198} {:answer/id #uuid "3f159693-7272-539b-8d07-778a3ed4938c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336624} {:answer/id #uuid "596e8a9c-9c2e-5135-a08e-ad772219a349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336849} {:answer/id #uuid "749562ba-1b02-5f83-a973-8bffa1c80cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337081} {:answer/id #uuid "de8038e5-bdf2-5d72-8003-0ca0304fe0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "596e8a9c-9c2e-5135-a08e-ad772219a349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336849} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f6508aa-26cc-5e67-a21e-e797c69eabff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8b7dd45b-b8d1-582b-9fbf-c50179d63d9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71221" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3{e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy|$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a0d643-3fba-5025-8ba0-fe80f13a0f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336191} {:answer/id #uuid "1a106852-4afd-52ed-ab68-75af9121d300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336273} {:answer/id #uuid "7079191a-2817-517c-b22b-ed0b7decbd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337033} {:answer/id #uuid "903c10f0-0ae9-5406-ba48-0978c42fa7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337339} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b520c6-d634-55fb-9a73-9aac376cbf2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a0d643-3fba-5025-8ba0-fe80f13a0f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336191} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc44ecd5-8b94-53f1-aa64-9fdcc88bcaac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3dcde73b-e9a7-56a8-9973-7e22f495be27" :question/math-academy-id "q-71234" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x(y-x)}{2x(y+1)}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,0) & =\\frac{(-1)(0-(-1))}{2(-1)(0+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,0) & ={f}_{y}(-1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $y-2z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,2,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4997e6e-db2a-59f0-8089-3716fd8ae7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383295} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b94256-12a4-5e4a-a7be-2faa95e83174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383296} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5682d4-84d4-5541-90d8-7e7040c8235e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383297} {:answer/id #uuid "1592e766-be1b-5bdf-ac2c-4264db8109b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383298} {:answer/id #uuid "749b8f4c-62f7-5c59-bc47-3128b8a6da53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa5682d4-84d4-5541-90d8-7e7040c8235e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383297} :answer-field/id #uuid "24582cee-9319-59e6-9a06-ddc0dc01268c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9055ebf-a856-5760-9126-377331881bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132578" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,5)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=4{x}^{2}y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,2,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,2).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2) & =4(1)^{2}(2)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd5e40ce-803c-5d96-872d-3f0e492eadbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383302} {:answer/id #uuid "720aed43-145e-53b9-bdfc-ad1cab577b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383303} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3769df-f295-584f-8d17-4cc6daf9c592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383304} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3cb4a3-a5c9-5109-9c27-f48151dd1ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383305} {:answer/id #uuid "42983750-aaf0-5078-93dc-54997c0e3fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb3769df-f295-584f-8d17-4cc6daf9c592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e22321c-1630-581b-a2cf-b182baccc9d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a3109e9-f31c-5d6f-a6b9-341111eed328" :question/math-academy-id "q-132580" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(e,2).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{e}=4-e\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7ce596-2d30-587f-8420-45e62e13d5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383309} {:answer/id #uuid "877b2fa4-194f-5fc4-8176-fe7bfbedcc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383310} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca68eba-afe1-5148-a902-7b75f1cf86fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383311} {:answer/id #uuid "625a56ce-727a-5b05-a3f7-9bccf25f5e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383312} {:answer/id #uuid "2f162d22-7bab-5326-997a-02d3e40fadea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "877b2fa4-194f-5fc4-8176-fe7bfbedcc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383310} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c8364fd-cb71-5621-b790-1b21e9be9448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c4b5aa7-27d5-5875-960f-3c04a61ab9fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132582" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(x+y){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\pi ).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\pi ) & =(1+\\pi ){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =(1+\\pi ){e}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ed7ce5-304c-5eac-b1cb-5f8c4b4d23e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,3,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383316} {:answer/id #uuid "4d34dabe-6639-51a1-abc1-40a551267c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383317} {:answer/id #uuid "7f96ce17-18b2-5a3f-8e28-936aabc05996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383318} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fe8dda-e437-53fb-9f60-09b8a339e900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383319} {:answer/id #uuid "650c5858-626f-56bb-bae0-7ffa49b7eab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d34dabe-6639-51a1-abc1-40a551267c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383317} :answer-field/id #uuid "51a3324e-1ddd-5ca6-b734-63094c92f926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d71daf7a-6da8-5e30-8340-64d86d4d3ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-132609" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & ={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)(y-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(y-1) \\\\ z & =2y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=2y-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-2$ (or $2y-z-2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6357e5c2-cf46-5660-bc20-49dbba19cc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385489} {:answer/id #uuid "c55a41e3-729e-57f0-b1aa-89cceb118ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385490} {:answer/id #uuid "7b13627d-a272-58c9-8614-2ccdaa4b3963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385491} {:answer/id #uuid "a48f0d5e-1c10-5086-b78b-c0af0a0d5cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385492} {:answer/id #uuid "e97a3c40-2850-570e-ac0a-9580b1fbaf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e97a3c40-2850-570e-ac0a-9580b1fbaf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385493} :answer-field/id #uuid "81978c17-aece-5d30-9a3d-6fc52afbe445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b3d97ced-0267-5370-864a-580ea21d98cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-71199" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $3$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}}{y-x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=3$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(3,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(3,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}}}{2-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9-8}}{-1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2187dab-e7ba-58a2-bb51-8cf8f2621861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385496} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b20770-5530-508f-960d-cfa8e2d86fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385497} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c3159c-2549-5a94-a72e-e70fd7aa61b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385498} {:answer/id #uuid "45723610-f576-57d8-968d-eb78eab9df33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385499} {:answer/id #uuid "f5cd429b-6112-5d6c-a161-6002e343448a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1c3159c-2549-5a94-a72e-e70fd7aa61b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385498} :answer-field/id #uuid "472f77ac-e8ea-5a7a-80e6-2f7ea6d432cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "851f656d-69a9-5c8d-8482-1385f4b7fe8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71224" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{2}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & ={f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)(y-0) \\\\ z-2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ (or $y+2z-4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-2,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df6297f-0710-55c1-aeca-d0bbd6193592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=5y+4\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436940} {:answer/id #uuid "46868586-3cca-540f-a185-1309d5aab5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=5x-14\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436942} {:answer/id #uuid "83e7b9f8-74d8-5b05-8940-fc35083f8350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=5x+6\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436945} {:answer/id #uuid "891f8685-7212-51ca-9476-5b254f301d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=-5x-6\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436946} {:answer/id #uuid "a94a09cf-cb2f-5cbc-9de1-d32e3178d6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=5x-6\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a94a09cf-cb2f-5cbc-9de1-d32e3178d6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=5x-6\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436947} :answer-field/id #uuid "2712fe89-2404-5ec9-a1bd-af26bb9d16b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "376329be-a379-553e-b714-516f831f127f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36372" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(2,0,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,0,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,0) & ={e}^{(0)}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,0)}(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,0,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=5x-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047539be-1b12-5b55-a1be-3e4cf9e127fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406792} {:answer/id #uuid "43cdbb4c-51d2-5f61-9d40-d04783c14422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406793} {:answer/id #uuid "60dae67c-4a44-58da-b15e-79beacb9c712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406794} {:answer/id #uuid "652341d7-abb3-51cc-926c-835644238288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406795} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f93202-6d15-5d8b-9e8d-6d60c947ab0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43cdbb4c-51d2-5f61-9d40-d04783c14422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406793} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b52ca0e-0824-536f-99e9-5701cb39c975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "320a3cd2-acfa-559d-b91b-9bfb6f0a3cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-71231" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =1(x-1) \\\\ z & =x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ z &=x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-1$ (or $x-z+-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f49be2f-9fbd-57d9-8743-c7f792e14929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407305} {:answer/id #uuid "40205211-9c82-5bbc-b29c-5211d1a45bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-2y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407306} {:answer/id #uuid "645649e7-f6ca-5dcf-a129-028646be2cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407308} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3f6706-e60e-5d15-b360-58385706ee4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-2y+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407309} {:answer/id #uuid "d4018ef0-2dba-5e3f-8527-3ea960bf4de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4018ef0-2dba-5e3f-8527-3ea960bf4de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407311} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94262ee-f4dd-5ac5-a015-9c5cf4798aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05b78370-0b37-5800-aa28-6b6b17964b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-71216" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,0,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425090} {:answer/id #uuid "2590094e-7f95-52df-bdd8-abb0af831110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425096} {:answer/id #uuid "336e9f14-d6c6-5f83-897b-4c14eb89e5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425097} {:answer/id #uuid "91000328-2b30-5049-9390-2fee6404bd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425109} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a190e1-968a-5e68-8061-84d550c81b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425090} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d50699f-3ed7-5626-8b63-c6ce285403be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5cb4c36d-f79b-5525-8cf7-91f3e27f40c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64966" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={2}^{x+y}y(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(0,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={2}^{0+1}(1)(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425119 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7b7abb-15fc-5e25-af71-ce99a58976f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425091} {:answer/id #uuid "14e7d503-39ad-5577-a641-1da1d8c70d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425094} {:answer/id #uuid "348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425098} {:answer/id #uuid "69c34c73-ac38-5363-8c2d-999f5d258274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425103} {:answer/id #uuid "759cdd15-f659-5676-8d91-9b242c2d2b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425098} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7802a6-4174-5690-9124-8f551b3466e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "666541af-0f77-5a3b-95c7-e3f70c0a6b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-71228" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)-(x+y)^{3}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)-(1+0)^{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =-1(y) \\\\ z+1 & =-y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425092} {:answer/id #uuid "1291615e-eaf6-5649-a612-304df178a8dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425093} {:answer/id #uuid "23478b53-a88f-568a-858a-7b25f93a7066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425095} {:answer/id #uuid "7733cd29-624f-51f2-ae97-a884afdbb38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425105} {:answer/id #uuid "e4854410-0525-5e63-82a6-354e8d687ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425092} :answer-field/id #uuid "472a8778-3d0c-5974-8ff7-bc1d5d8b12f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d880e565-bd50-5672-98e1-45142968e59d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71226" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=5.$ Given that the point $P(4,5,-2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(4,5).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,5) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}{2(4)-(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(4,5) & ={f}_{x}(4,5)(x-4) \\\\ z-(-2) & =1(x-4) \\\\ z+2 & =x-4 \\\\ z & =x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,5,-2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=5 \\\\ z &=x-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=5,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-6$ (or $x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1e7001-fa28-52e7-9159-e1a9952ad01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436941} {:answer/id #uuid "5212a9fa-3e07-5070-b235-7a3b34cb5bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436943} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba5571e-63b9-59ee-98ea-36a27b8aac00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436944} {:answer/id #uuid "cc662000-df0c-5f2c-a156-05cd36f47ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436948} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a14c70-f4ee-5c8e-bd4a-ccddfe87a87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5212a9fa-3e07-5070-b235-7a3b34cb5bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436943} :answer-field/id #uuid "aee200cc-d9b2-5d5b-a23a-38088da4f688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0063a739-db3e-5983-b65e-bb4c57fabf9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64967" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\pi $ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\pi ,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\pi ,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\pi ,1) & =(1){e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022e71c7-1d96-5b12-ab9b-3c65246aca6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=2x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499657} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecbc247-a7b4-51ad-ac21-7f50b73d7f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499803} {:answer/id #uuid "4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499806} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf544a9-31d0-5386-9054-024015e9e1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=3x-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500070} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fec6bc-c021-533e-ab85-5127fbbe71f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=2x+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499806} :answer-field/id #uuid "1450b1c6-e44c-5c3f-b403-2b991fbf5f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac629eca-77ed-5bb7-99e7-49845bdc5d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71204" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(y+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0)\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10528982-819d-558d-b7b9-c606e71872b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=3x+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514906} {:answer/id #uuid "7d86ddc1-2c7d-548c-9b05-047b8457b098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=x-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515131} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e89105-6501-5255-9bbc-617aef43299e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=x-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515228} {:answer/id #uuid "becf689a-2251-5072-95a8-008d1c2e4200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=2x+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515277} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b5a29-3a47-5678-8b2a-9278853cd35b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=2x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b5a29-3a47-5678-8b2a-9278853cd35b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=2x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515373} :answer-field/id #uuid "37d670d1-3bff-5409-be4d-e79488473006" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae3534f1-44d3-55be-bcdb-7634e16c97c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132594" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2x{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=2x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311d0b20-5465-53bf-8af8-033f8a691b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-4 \\\\ z &=- 8 x - 17\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518850} {:answer/id #uuid "4c90283e-17c2-5956-a222-1db0ed4abe72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-4 \\\\ z &=15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518858} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5473db-40c3-50b6-8ddd-7b6cadb61d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-4 \\\\ z &=16 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518890} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b88192-c899-53c8-837d-bdb7ab15dfa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=- 8 y - 33\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518892} {:answer/id #uuid "da098dab-79f4-58a3-80e8-513f4358580d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-4 \\\\ z &=8 x - 17\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c90283e-17c2-5956-a222-1db0ed4abe72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-4 \\\\ z &=15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518858} :answer-field/id #uuid "e73da421-63d8-5f13-81f5-0f4c7d6bdcd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "03efd4b4-041a-5564-818d-06a45aad1a76" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-4.$ Given that the point $P(2,-4,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=- 4 x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,-4,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-4) & =-4(2) \\\\ & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,-4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-8)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,-4,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-4 \\\\ z &=15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cf7030-0c6b-58ec-b96b-cc1eaee16f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518837} {:answer/id #uuid "2350b4de-0a61-567a-8e6e-86269a60e405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518843} {:answer/id #uuid "a3381c9b-b21a-5c98-a1cc-92e5b24f891c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=\\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518881} {:answer/id #uuid "bcef6f07-ae1f-515e-9823-933456ed0e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x - 2\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518888} {:answer/id #uuid "eb70d200-f386-5ec6-b52c-68c76a65bcc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(y + \\pi\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2350b4de-0a61-567a-8e6e-86269a60e405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518843} :answer-field/id #uuid "8840c5cb-d522-528d-9760-41089c81f54c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0bc337d9-050a-554a-8d77-360e1bc2538f" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Given that the point $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-2,- \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,- \\pi) & =(- \\pi)\\cos((-2)(- \\pi))+\\cos(- \\pi) \\\\ & =(- \\pi)(1)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =- \\pi - 1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(9) & =(- \\pi - 1)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z & =- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c129fb-2de3-52fe-aa21-6bb037b5ed9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=6 y + 15\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518863} {:answer/id #uuid "7d447cec-93f4-5f7f-866b-3cc5460bed1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=6 x + 21\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518871} {:answer/id #uuid "7f84a2d7-a0be-5d0a-b257-029f139cfa5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=6 x - 18\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518872} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a6e425-12c9-56ec-9462-10549818c4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=21 - 6 x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518885} {:answer/id #uuid "cc41e06d-cc47-5ae9-87ea-bc466f3262a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/r