urface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Given that the point $P(2,- \\pi,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,- \\pi,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,- \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,- \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,- \\pi) & =(- \\pi)\\cos((2)(- \\pi))+\\cos(- \\pi) \\\\ & =(- \\pi)(1.0)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =- 1.0 \\pi - 1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,- \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,- \\pi)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(1) & =(- \\pi - 1)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =- \\pi x - x + 3 + 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,- \\pi,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\pi x - x + 3 + 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0835f080-946f-5479-8136-b72dc3e8f778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- 3 \\pi \\\\ z &=\\left(1 + 3 \\pi\\right) \\left(x - 2\\right) + 5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518839} {:answer/id #uuid "444a15db-f996-5afb-9dc1-b5dbd979fb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- 3 \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 + 3 \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right) + 5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518854} {:answer/id #uuid "4f40d0bb-59df-580a-a14d-05979cea1db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/