nd{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 16 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,16⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c44812f-aa8f-5a3b-a2c9-3a9af8c5a229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556143} {:answer/id #uuid "9d66afe9-4f16-5fcb-ad94-4581dad49b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556147} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7a8a71-8f71-5f9a-b3fd-db2af683847a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556149} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4ef977-ff0a-57f1-b6ad-8ad2f3a7abed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556156} {:answer/id #uuid "e85558f7-5bb9-5e70-8631-2dd61ed3184f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d66afe9-4f16-5fcb-ad94-4581dad49b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556147} :answer-field/id #uuid "39fe770b-a34b-5bb0-b24e-2cb8ef45a74d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd9d8206-b563-5e86-ab9a-f167bb3c1e2b" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(3)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =(3)(-2)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(-6)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =- 6 x - 12 \\\\ z & =- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 6 x - 16$ (or $- 6 x - z - 16=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,6⟩)=⟨1,0,-6⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13415e5b-d7ce-5839-ac8a-9fdda0db113d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556109} {:answer/id #uuid "4abd2572-3ff6-52e7-b470-d37775dc5e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556120} {:answer/id #uuid "58a13d5a-69b9-5fed-8522-be2e66a2c2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556123} {:answer/id #uuid "88e51073-51d9-5a66-b57f-a46e981e04ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556141} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d2fa4a-ce82-5f44-aa09-5b63b8171852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4abd2572-3ff6-52e7-b470-d37775dc5e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556120} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff0d9c98-1039-5b45-b75d-19a75d93883f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5e09c79-be5e-59c8-bad2-508a4b285ff7" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =- 2 x - 4 \\\\ z & =- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 2 x - 4$ (or $- 2 x - z - 4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i + 2 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,2⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,2⟩)=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044116f0-96fa-511a-a093-d3f451857cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556103} {:answer/id #uuid "1882e685-d6cd-57e2-8865-032f24c20593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,6,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556110} {:answer/id #uuid "252f91f1-8db7-563d-92ee-dfc8b2e40f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556112} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb507d4-3fd6-5419-b985-3cf7868f4344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556127} {:answer/id #uuid "f9241366-8977-556e-a838-84ae8f1a7a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb507d4-3fd6-5419-b985-3cf7868f4344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556127} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a486d1b-902f-5597-a6ae-36fd96e6212e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f350a280-28d9-5c60-b2e4-617d820b96d3" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Given that the point $P(-3,3,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(2)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,3) & =(2)(3)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-3,3) & ={f}_{y}(-3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =6 y - 18 \\\\ z & =6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-3,3,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=-3 \\\\ z=6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6 y - 19$ (or $6 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,6⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a92a5b0-f742-5f72-9fed-2fa17746b3d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563076} {:answer/id #uuid "e312bb41-87f9-5deb-9841-5b5f28418294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563445} {:answer/id #uuid "e72b0968-76fc-5d45-85c7-a1f5300ad455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563457} {:answer/id #uuid "fa10a00d-f989-5e31-96e2-3b5966e8e36b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563506} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7c1f10-9c81-523f-bfa3-f0d613a444d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e312bb41-87f9-5deb-9841-5b5f28418294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563445} :answer-field/id #uuid "3db3d25b-6794-5819-bf20-9d7f989372fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "19ce6755-ce5c-5344-86b9-dca6271615dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132608" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(y+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0) & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $x-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e6dbddc-14bc-525e-8243-43d97f13d5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563160} {:answer/id #uuid "647eb414-70e2-51df-bace-381aeabfab1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563176} {:answer/id #uuid "8af33f3e-47b0-5222-adb2-0e369970c083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563255} {:answer/id #uuid "a03c2c09-7f10-51f5-b6e9-049c1b7317c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563305} {:answer/id #uuid "c9bdd2b2-810c-5ef0-84b0-8ece0783d384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "647eb414-70e2-51df-bace-381aeabfab1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563176} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd0c0893-97b7-5c87-8979-00978834031e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4e141666-84d5-5edd-9a4e-a7cb3e788f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-132581" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df674f5-22d1-57e9-8542-daec656270b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563010} {:answer/id #uuid "1f196182-837a-5bce-a183-aadf810b7f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=2x-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563015} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8b41aa-822d-5816-ad83-338eb5cc1486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=x-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563120} {:answer/id #uuid "54faae86-df4c-5126-b41f-7bacd23c8482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563139} {:answer/id #uuid "76b72c1f-3222-52aa-ba57-d8ef2a3b29a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=2x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b72c1f-3222-52aa-ba57-d8ef2a3b29a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=2x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563206} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ed29c1-ef21-57a0-9839-78668465edfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd4791a4-a5ea-5e12-b1c8-f45a53372bd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71214" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5db68e00-0fe1-52aa-9d6b-1339f0b2d290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570288} {:answer/id #uuid "71ba467b-f837-5940-861f-031a2bb1bdfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570327} {:answer/id #uuid "968d7b90-c1b7-584f-85fa-c61f8ad5605f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570386} {:answer/id #uuid "97246dec-ee18-59de-9d5f-9526af928b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570389} {:answer/id #uuid "aae9a8b0-d1ae-5e09-af38-db030076ac47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "968d7b90-c1b7-584f-85fa-c61f8ad5605f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570386} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1aab5e-5cef-5890-b716-82e71a46cb8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4cb48e08-10b0-5815-97ab-c688f0460cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132606" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{(1/2)} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & ={f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2})(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4y-2 \\\\ z & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4y-1$ (or $4y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220110a8-4d5f-54c2-8842-59572152098d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585952} {:answer/id #uuid "affab07d-9546-5794-9cac-5c0fc4c6e00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586232} {:answer/id #uuid "c7152b2f-f816-5439-b852-4757b9c623a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586268} {:answer/id #uuid "ef10e3a9-8a06-5ff3-bffe-dfc28c13936b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586347} {:answer/id #uuid "efb4979f-ace1-50b7-bfc1-405046255aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "affab07d-9546-5794-9cac-5c0fc4c6e00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586232} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ef0e07-1abe-51f1-9889-a23013093e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "62b94792-daa5-5cde-af61-1e88da21b8ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-71196" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{y}}{2x}+2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,1,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1) & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{1}}{2(1)}+2(1) \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5418525a-2c4e-5b58-bdc4-f6fc67aa7d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707124} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac2ffe6-5cde-5f01-b4a4-f6d1a97df1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707126} {:answer/id #uuid "912c575f-de7f-5265-9ccc-3a42a582a826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707130} {:answer/id #uuid "9a78cfdb-56b6-5e71-8e3f-5cbe63c79698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707131} {:answer/id #uuid "de4b0e27-1b9a-5ca6-93c3-506f3eb3f324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "912c575f-de7f-5265-9ccc-3a42a582a826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707130} :answer-field/id #uuid "199b24d6-f7ac-5977-a699-4dc9366fcac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2645b7b5-976e-5563-b16a-938d6a17a8c2" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=-1$. Given that $P(-1,2,4)$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=3x-2y,\n$$\n\nfind the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane $x=-1$ holds $x$ constant, so $y$ varies along the section. Its tangent slope is $dz/dy=f_y(-1,2)$.\n\nSubstitute the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=f_y(-1,2)\\\\\n &=3(-1)-2(2)\\\\\n &=-3-4\\\\\n &=-7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe height $z=4$ locates the point on the surface; it is not an input to $f_y(x,y)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202f8212-213c-53da-bec2-b9adf121f5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "202f8212-213c-53da-bec2-b9adf121f5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707122} :answer-field/id #uuid "903e0cfa-04a0-56d5-9d94-5e3dd0581570" :answer-field/key "slope" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c2cc273-1adc-59a9-b88f-7db9b624a378" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=f(x,y)=12-3x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2$. The diagram shows this section, with $x$ horizontal and $z$ vertical.\n\n![](images/d5/d57e0d9e2dddbbfa44a0545f83aac1e2c4626d552fbb2fbd71983840e498f0d9.png)\n\nFind the slope $m=dz/dx$ of the tangent to $C$ at $P(1,2,5)$.\n\n$m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=2$ holds $y$ constant, so the slope of this section is given by $f_x$, not $f_y$.\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=0-6x-0\\\\\n &=-6x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, $x=1$, so\n\n$$\nm=f_x(1,2)=-6(1)=-6.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using the section itself: $g(x)=f(x,2)=8-3x^2$, and $g'(1)=-6$. The curve descends as $x$ increases through $1$, consistent with the negative slope."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0826d645-0980-51b8-b1b6-b1d2500eb726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707119} {:answer/id #uuid "0c27ba08-befb-5e6d-a6dc-17c78d1b9212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2+e^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707120} {:answer/id #uuid "70a2d358-03c4-5a19-aba4-d6bdb109f150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{e^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707125} {:answer/id #uuid "8b659c75-52a0-5318-b6ed-f1fb52f7aefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{e^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707127} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cd0e01-3de6-5cc8-a55c-ca2b46b8995b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{e^2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70a2d358-03c4-5a19-aba4-d6bdb109f150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{e^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707125} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a569b9-4668-51e1-9615-27d025cc1073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c52c2989-a4c7-5f33-b736-4a7df9a511a0" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined, for $y>0$, by\n\n$$\nz=f(x,y)=x\\ln y+\\frac{e^{xy}}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1$. Find the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at the point $P$ whose $y$-coordinate is $2$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x=1$ is fixed, $y$ varies along $C$. The required slope is $f_y(1,2)$.\n\nHolding $x$ constant, differentiate $x\\ln y$ and use the quotient rule on $e^{xy}/y$. The chain rule gives $\\partial(e^{xy})/\\partial y=xe^{xy}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y(xe^{xy})-e^{xy}}{y^2}\\\\\n &=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{e^{xy}(xy-1)}{y^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $(x,y)=(1,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_y(1,2)&=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{e^2(2-1)}{4}\\\\\n &=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{e^2}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a check, the section is $g(y)=\\ln y+e^y/y$. Its derivative is $g'(y)=1/y+e^y/y-e^y/y^2$, which gives the same slope at $y=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73fd5b7e-0b0a-528e-9900-77c9a851808f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=4y-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707211} {:answer/id #uuid "7f565330-0cf3-582c-ab84-e2e9188d4649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=-4y-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707214} {:answer/id #uuid "b416791d-fa4b-511c-af84-3bb6b6c45b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=-2y+9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707216} {:answer/id #uuid "d50c58d2-5b03-530d-86b3-271dd6fbfea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=-4y+5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707218} {:answer/id #uuid "e93442ee-09d6-576b-979c-b6a83fc03975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=-4y+13\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e93442ee-09d6-576b-979c-b6a83fc03975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=-4y+13\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707221} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec633ab0-e796-5c56-8b5a-48bb6d2e0883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e19cb945-377e-56c1-bac9-339d66ed6667" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)=18-x^2-y^2$ with the plane $x=3$. The point $P(3,2,5)$ lies on $C$, and $f_y=-2y$.\n\n![The surface cut by the plane x = 3, with its intersection curve and tangent at P.](images/86/8619c41cd0caf04ae9ee9bcae61e31739d8da583793a67c1c87e00186c3ac417.png)\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$.\n\nUse $m=f_y(3,2)$ and $z-z_0=m(y-y_0)$, keeping $x=3$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is fixed, $y$ varies along the section. Its tangent slope is\n\n$$\nm=f_y(3,2)=-2(2)=-4.\n$$\n\nUse the point $(y_0,z_0)=(2,5)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-5&=-4(y-2) \\\\ z&=-4y+13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent remains in the plane $x=3$, so the line is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=-4y+13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAs a check, the section is $z=9-y^2$. At $y=2$ its height is $5$ and its derivative is $-4$, agreeing with the tangent shown below.\n\n![The parabolic section in the yz coordinates and its tangent at P.](images/e9/e982ef5d6d8d4fec9c64957d46f22cea2a85bd002595642f58700cf48cce4256.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a587dc59-27a0-5c3e-8e0b-0bfbbb25bc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a587dc59-27a0-5c3e-8e0b-0bfbbb25bc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707215} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47b930e-c1ef-510c-bdeb-863b91d71944" :answer-field/key "plane" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707217} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a4f6c39-3301-5d62-a24e-8de1f341b5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)y+1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a4f6c39-3301-5d62-a24e-8de1f341b5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)y+1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707212} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7693474-999a-5eac-a232-1ea0680114a4" :answer-field/key "height" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecc7abe1-ce57-535f-91d5-75e9d448ee61" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e$. The point $P(e,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=\\frac{\\ln x}{\\sqrt{y+1}}+\\frac{e^y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$. Give exact answers, with $z$ expressed in terms of $y$.\n\n$x=$\n{{plane}}\n\n$z=$\n{{height}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=e$ is fixed, use $f_y$ for the tangent slope. Evaluating at $(e,1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=f_y(e,1)&=\\frac{\\ln e}{\\sqrt{1+1}}+\\frac{e^1}{e} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply point-slope form at $(y_0,z_0)=(1,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2&=\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)(y-1) \\\\ z&=\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)y+1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)y+1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAt $y=1$ the height is $2$, and the coefficient of $y$ equals $f_y(e,1)$. The equivalent expression $2+(1+1/\\sqrt{2})(y-1)$ is also valid for the second blank."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e36f9b-3031-5f60-a0b6-2c8c90a45298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-1 \\\\ z &=x+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707207} {:answer/id #uuid "10aa57ce-1df6-516a-8a99-10f34d2cb021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-1 \\\\ z &=-2x+3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707208} {:answer/id #uuid "45c5655c-55d4-54f7-b289-c13b57c6462e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-1 \\\\ z &=2x-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707209} {:answer/id #uuid "5be8d7aa-d10c-5fe9-bbf7-d2677cd8f86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-1 \\\\ z &=-x+5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707210} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e131a4-4157-5af0-9f4b-eba8d93c1f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-1 \\\\ z &=-2x+7\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e131a4-4157-5af0-9f4b-eba8d93c1f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-1 \\\\ z &=-2x+7\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707224} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf37dd9-5375-5668-a7fb-9e715b123765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbd766d2-e18d-5c6d-b4ca-11114d18ec23" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-1$. Given that $P(2,-1,3)$ lies on $C$ and that $f_x=y\\sqrt{x+2}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane fixes $y$, so the tangent slope is $f_x(2,-1)$, measured as change in $z$ per change in $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m&=(-1)\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\ &=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $(x_0,z_0)=(2,3)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3&=-2(x-2) \\\\ z&=-2x+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTogether with the fixed coordinate, the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-1 \\\\ z &=-2x+7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAt $x=2$, this gives $z=3$; its slope is $-2$, as required."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c3dda6-6d73-5b17-b7a6-c8ce2cadd4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 1,0,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707327} {:answer/id #uuid "59526181-703d-5f46-b50d-ebcd419ef1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,1,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707329} {:answer/id #uuid "63c449f4-10a1-5a76-8d68-9efe5aa197ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 1,0,2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707331} {:answer/id #uuid "786b215c-ec72-5f94-90ef-bf9dd0898a42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 3,0,1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707335} {:answer/id #uuid "be75f1d6-7af1-5989-9edf-5138470f3566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 1,0,-3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c3dda6-6d73-5b17-b7a6-c8ce2cadd4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 1,0,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707327} :answer-field/id #uuid "241066ad-966b-5acd-b9d7-1adf5cef81c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "04ac610c-1e7f-57cd-aa40-8682d06ac837" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z=f(x,y)$, with the plane $y=-1$. The point $P(2,-1,3)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=2x+y.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=-1$ holds $y$ fixed, so $x$ varies and the slope in this slice is $f_x(2,-1)$:\n\n$$\nm=f_x(2,-1)=2(2)+(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nA change of $1$ in $x$ along the tangent gives a change of $3$ in $z$, with no change in $y$. Hence $\\langle 1,0,3\\rangle$ is a tangent vector.\n\nUsing the point-slope equation, the tangent line through $P(2,-1,3)$ is the intersection\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y&=-1 \\\\ z-3&=3(x-2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second plane can be written $3x-z-3=0$. Its normal is $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 3,0,-1\\rangle$, while a normal to $y=-1$ is $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 0,1,0\\rangle$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2\n=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 0&1&0 \\\\ 3&0&-1\\end{vmatrix}\n=\\langle -1,0,-3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nIts negative is the listed vector $\\langle 1,0,3\\rangle$. The component ratio is $v_z/v_x=3$; $\\langle 3,0,1\\rangle$ would instead give slope $1/3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afff314a-db96-50fc-8e88-bbac48b22be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,-1,\\sqrt{3}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707339} {:answer/id #uuid "bbabab4f-350a-5742-8ec0-7523a09d74e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,-\\sqrt{3},1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707340} {:answer/id #uuid "c25089e1-cfc3-542f-a930-c7587dfdf27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,\\sqrt{3},1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707342} {:answer/id #uuid "d0942042-5356-5355-ab34-22c818f20d7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,1,-\\sqrt{6}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707344} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a7e359-69b8-5e3e-8a06-6470a22151e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,-\\sqrt{3},2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbabab4f-350a-5742-8ec0-7523a09d74e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,-\\sqrt{3},1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707340} :answer-field/id #uuid "781e8f4e-3380-51eb-99a0-0dd3e188e1d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "639e8d19-14ec-57da-8612-221b97e0ae48" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z=f(x,y)$, with the plane $x=-2$. The point $P(-2,-4,1)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "The plane $x=-2$ holds $x$ fixed, so the slope in the slice is $f_y(-2,-4)$. Evaluating the given partial derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\frac{-4-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-4+(-2))}} \\\\ &=\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{12}} \\\\ &=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA tangent vector is therefore $\\langle 0,1,-1/\\sqrt{3}\\rangle$. Multiplying by the nonzero scalar $-\\sqrt{3}$ gives the listed vector $\\langle 0,-\\sqrt{3},1\\rangle$. Its ratio of $z$-change to $y$-change is $1/(-\\sqrt{3})=-1/\\sqrt{3}$.\n\nThe tangent line through $P(-2,-4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=-2 \\\\ z-1&=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(y+4)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nRearranging the second plane gives $y+\\sqrt{3}z+4-\\sqrt{3}=0$. Normal vectors to these planes are $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 1,0,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 0,1,\\sqrt{3}\\rangle$. Their cross product confirms the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2\n=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 1&0&0 \\\\ 0&1&\\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix}\n=\\langle 0,-\\sqrt{3},1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nBoth signs of a direction vector are allowed, but the component ratio must be preserved. For example, $\\langle 0,-1,\\sqrt{3}\\rangle$ has slope $-\\sqrt{3}$, so it is not parallel to this tangent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "664be6da-4347-58c0-a8ef-777c42b1529d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,1,-4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707332} {:answer/id #uuid "751f56fb-bfda-5083-8435-28517411dcd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,0,-4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707333} {:answer/id #uuid "818fe346-c142-5a7d-8817-09ef37286adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,1,4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707336} {:answer/id #uuid "9030bd14-e0e9-5134-95b4-57f4820d5bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,4,-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707337} {:answer/id #uuid "ada9b61b-f238-5af4-8cd4-0bfc5ee73158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 1,0,-4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "664be6da-4347-58c0-a8ef-777c42b1529d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 0,1,-4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707332} :answer-field/id #uuid "07c17f19-be46-5711-9bbf-cd5709af18a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf5fa440-5ad2-5603-8696-c92ed82ecb49" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)=18-x^2-y^2$ with the plane $x=3$. The point $P(3,2,5)$ lies on $C$.\n\n![The surface cut by the plane x=3, with the intersection curve and its tangent at P.](images/86/8619c41cd0caf04ae9ee9bcae61e31739d8da583793a67c1c87e00186c3ac417.png)\n\nAt $P$, the slope in this slice is $f_y(3,2)=-4$. Because $x$ is fixed, a tangent vector has $x$-component $0$.\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=3$ is fixed, $y$ varies along the curve. The slope $dz/dy=-4$ means that a change of $1$ in $y$ along the tangent corresponds to a change of $-4$ in $z$. Thus a tangent vector is $\\langle 0,1,-4\\rangle$.\n\nThe tangent line through $P(3,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=3 \\\\ z-5&=-4(y-2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe slice $z=9-y^2$ and its tangent are shown below. The horizontal coordinate is $y$; the tangent slopes downward as $y$ increases.\n\n![The yz slice z=9-y squared and the downward-sloping tangent at P.](images/e9/e982ef5d6d8d4fec9c64957d46f22cea2a85bd002595642f58700cf48cce4256.png)\n\nTo connect this with the plane-intersection method, rewrite the second equation as $4y+z-13=0$. Normal vectors to the two planes are $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 1,0,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 0,4,1\\rangle$. Their cross product is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2\n=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 1&0&0 \\\\ 0&4&1\\end{vmatrix}\n=\\langle 0,-1,4\\rangle.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$ gives $\\langle 0,1,-4\\rangle$, the listed tangent vector. Either direction along the tangent is valid; its $z$-change divided by its $y$-change must equal $-4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903cf713-5358-509f-b648-8a5c065711d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "903cf713-5358-509f-b648-8a5c065711d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707465} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86403c8-003e-5ece-9140-401b63273940" :answer-field/key "slope" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "012565e5-bca5-5f1f-92a5-7e81efea8ce8" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $x=1$. Given that $P(1,2,3)$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $y$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $x=1$ holds $x$ constant, so $y$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dy=f_y(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_y(1,2)&=\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(2)^{2}}+(2)\\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{4}+2(1) \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe height $3$ is not an input to the supplied partial derivative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc9fa25-bab2-5a27-8bcd-b2307006c3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebc9fa25-bab2-5a27-8bcd-b2307006c3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707473} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6a8fa22-0db9-56c4-990a-901eaca148f5" :answer-field/key "slope" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f41ebc7-0fdb-5f3b-9895-7cec5b922f95" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $y=2$. Given that $P(0,2,-7)$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)={4}^{x+y}y(x\\ln (4)+1).\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $x$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=2$ holds $y$ constant, so $x$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dx=f_x(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_x(0,2)&={4}^{0+2}(2)(0\\cdot \\ln (4)+1)=32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe height $-7$ is not an input to the supplied partial derivative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "996dcc8d-8005-557f-909e-03929aa72a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996dcc8d-8005-557f-909e-03929aa72a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707466} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9dcaee7-096a-5470-96ae-096f3c3c80a1" :answer-field/key "slope" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "473aa500-088e-5b8b-8782-371b0c5aacb6" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $y=1$. Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\pi$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=y e^{\\sin(xy)}\\cos(xy).\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $x$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=1$ holds $y$ constant, so $x$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dx=f_x(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_x(\\pi,1)&=(1)e^{\\sin((\\pi)(1))}\\cos((\\pi)(1)) \\\\\n&=(1)e^{0}(-1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "356179d8-8cce-5049-af97-66195dc13091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "356179d8-8cce-5049-af97-66195dc13091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707460} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d9391d-df13-57b4-91a1-2534ae8598de" :answer-field/key "slope" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "75a1ce50-36a6-5f83-b7bc-c355f8d4af52" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $y=\\pi$. Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=y\\cos x + \\cos y.\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $x$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=\\pi$ holds $y$ constant, so $x$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dx=f_x(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_x(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\pi)&=(\\pi)\\cos \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)+\\cos (\\pi) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1489558-d0fa-5a54-82a0-6f5aaf52e2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1489558-d0fa-5a54-82a0-6f5aaf52e2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707468} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea786041-85e1-50fd-ae47-93d2b467ffd5" :answer-field/key "slope" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bec4f45d-feb5-5233-b697-6cc0ec1b3abc" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $y=3$. Given that $P(-3,3,2)$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $x$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=3$ holds $y$ constant, so $x$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dx=f_x(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_x(-3,3)&=5(-3)^{2}(3)=135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe height $2$ is not an input to the supplied partial derivative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acde688-986f-5a41-b3e1-729747af8dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=4 x - 24\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707655} {:answer/id #uuid "360aaf0c-0f5a-5f1e-8215-b7ddbcffca22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=2 x - 24\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707663} {:answer/id #uuid "3c363c79-fcda-59d7-94b8-e43edb6d0c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=4 x - 14\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707665} {:answer/id #uuid "60fe26f1-485c-5fee-9bba-e258b90a20d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=2 x - 14\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707670} {:answer/id #uuid "cc46f97d-e664-5d53-9e97-05ea334a5728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=20 - 4 x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1acde688-986f-5a41-b3e1-729747af8dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=4 x - 24\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707655} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a575b8e-f1ec-5068-a6ab-2c89aebd9092" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3229d511-7bae-57b7-bada-f70af7b205cc" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2$. The point $P(5,-2,-4)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=-\\left(y\\sqrt{\\left(x - 1\\right)}\\right)\n$$\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane fixes $y$, so $x$ varies along the section. Evaluate the supplied partial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m&=f_x(5,-2) \\\\ &=-\\left(\\left(-2\\right)\\sqrt{\\left(\\left(5\\right) - 1\\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form at $(x_0,z_0)=(5,-4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-(-4)&=(4)(x-(5)) \\\\ z&=4 x - 24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent retains the fixed-coordinate plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=4 x - 24\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5cca12-3b19-5421-9d88-790702b6a221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=- 8 y - 5 + 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707656} {:answer/id #uuid "289becc4-b8e1-50ae-843f-bb6ae0483f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=- 4 y - 5 + 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707657} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd46d57-fdf0-5d81-8ca8-450d6df4db6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4 y - 5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707660} 